== Structural audit ==

All 10 Core pages use [[Template:Knowledge object]], [[Template:Reading depth]], source status, references, controlled Domain values, and Core-owned category/semantic identity.

All 20 Simple/Technical derivatives use [[Template:Reading depth]], declare source status, contain sources/references, and do not duplicate Core Knowledge object/category identity.

== Title / identity audit ==

The existing request [[Reproducible bioinformatics workflow]] is normalized to [[Reproducible computational research]].

This is not a duplicate of [[Reproducibility]]:

<pre>
Reproducibility
= general property/goal that a result can be reproduced under stated conditions

Reproducible computational research
= concrete practices and artifacts for regenerating computational results
</pre>

No other Wave 12 title requires qualification.

== Domain audit ==

No new controlled Domain is required.

== Genetics / genomics audit ==

[[Genetics]] retains heredity/gene/variation scope.

[[Genomics]] retains genome-scale scope and is not treated as a synonym for genetics or genome sequencing.

Neither page adopts deterministic language implying that genetic influence alone fixes complex traits.

== Bioinformatics / data audit ==

[[Bioinformatics]] remains an interdisciplinary computational-biology field rather than “anything done with computers in biology.”

[[Biological sequence]] separates symbolic sequence data from the physical molecule and from annotation.

[[Biological database]] separates database infrastructure/records from factual truth and records version/provenance as scientific concerns.

[[Sequence annotation]] separates predicted, curated, transferred, and experimentally supported claims.

== Alignment / phylogenetics audit ==

[[Sequence alignment]] explicitly preserves:

<pre>
alignment ≠ homology
similarity ≠ homology
best score ≠ guaranteed biological truth
</pre>

[[Phylogenetics]] treats trees as inferred models with uncertainty. Gene-tree/species-tree non-equivalence remains explicit.

== Systems-biology audit ==

[[Systems biology]] integrates experiment and quantitative/computational models of biological interactions and dynamics. It is not defined by network visualization alone.

The page reuses [[Network]], [[Feedback]], [[Complex system]], [[System boundary]], and [[Scientific model]] without redefining them.

== Reproducible-computation audit ==

[[Reproducible computational research]] is deliberately broader than bioinformatics while remaining a strong bridge back to it.

Required artifacts/practices include identified data, code/workflow versions, parameters/seeds, dependencies/environment, execution order, provenance, validation and documentation.

The page explicitly rejects:

<pre>
open code alone = reproducible analysis
computational reproducibility = independent replication
bit-for-bit identity = only valid reproducibility criterion
</pre>

== Request-lifecycle audit ==

The seven near-term Bioinformatics request records transition from requested to satisfied.

Six map directly to same-title canonical pages.

“Reproducible bioinformatics workflow” maps to [[Reproducible computational research]] with the normalization recorded rather than creating a redundant narrower identity.

== Cross-wave audit ==

Wave 12 connects cleanly to the prior corpus:

* [[Causality]], [[Probability]], [[Measurement]], [[Uncertainty]], and [[Scientific model]] remain general inference foundations;
* [[Algorithm]], [[Database]], [[Metadata]], [[Software]], [[Version control]], and [[Provenance]] remain computing/data foundations;
* [[Information]], [[Network]], [[Feedback]], [[Complex system]], and [[Adaptation (systems)]] retain their general systems meanings;
* [[Reproducibility]], [[Replication]], and [[Validation]] are reused rather than duplicated.

No local personal research project or one biological subfield defines a public concept.

